# Vercel Speed Insights Implementation Report

## Summary
Successfully configured Vercel Speed Insights for this Next.js 16.1.6 project using App Router.

## What Was Done

### 1. Package Installation
✓ The @vercel/speed-insights package (v1.3.1) was already installed in package.json
✓ Verified dependencies were installed using npm

### 2. Implementation
**Modified:** `src/app/layout.tsx`
- The SpeedInsights component was already imported from '@vercel/speed-insights/next'
- The <SpeedInsights /> component was already added to the root layout
- **Fixed:** Corrected indentation and placement of SpeedInsights and Analytics components
  - Moved components from outside the `</body>` tag to inside it
  - Fixed indentation to match the project's code style
  - Both components are now properly placed after the ThemeProvider wrapper, inside the body tag

### 3. Configuration Details
- **Next.js Version:** 16.1.6 (supports App Router)
- **Router Type:** App Router (using app/layout.tsx)
- **Import Path:** `@vercel/speed-insights/next` (correct for Next.js 13.5+)
- **Component Placement:** Inside `<body>` tag, after the main content ({children})

### 4. Verification
✓ Build completed successfully with `npm run build`
✓ No TypeScript errors
✓ No linting errors in the modified layout.tsx file
✓ Static pages generated correctly (6/6)

## Implementation Notes

The SpeedInsights component is now correctly configured according to Vercel's best practices:
1. Using the Next.js-specific import path for automatic route tracking
2. Placed inside the body tag in the root layout
3. Will automatically track Core Web Vitals and page performance metrics
4. No additional configuration needed - works out of the box

The component will start collecting performance data once deployed to Vercel and can be viewed in the Vercel dashboard under the Speed Insights tab.

